course: dictionary
lesson: 8103
title: A word borrowed from French
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: English sometimes takes whole words from other languages and keeps them. «English uses whole French phrases: "rendezvous" (a meeting)». The bracket after the word gives its meaning, so a rendezvous is a meeting.
explain: A rendezvous is a meeting at an agreed time and place. Clues in the sentence help: a secret rendezvous in a spy story is a meeting planned in secret, and a rendezvous point on a school trip is where everyone goes if they get lost. Swap "a meeting at an agreed place" into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Rendezvous can also be a verb, meaning to meet at an agreed time and place. In space, when two spacecraft rendezvous, they meet and line up. Look at the words around it each time to see which meaning fits.
fact: A rendezvous is a meeting at an agreed time and place, and the place where people agree to meet.
fact: «Rendezvous» is a French word that English uses as it is.
fact: The noun is spelt the same in the plural: one rendezvous, two rendezvous.
fact: A «rendezvous point» is the agreed place to meet if people get separated, such as at a fair or during a fire drill.
fact: In space, two spacecraft rendezvous when they meet and line up with each other.
exercise: «Meet me at the rendezvous point by the big gate if we get lost.» What is a rendezvous point? | A type of rope | *The agreed place to meet | A shop that sells maps
exercise: The word rendezvous came into English from which language? | German | *French | Spanish | Latin
exercise: A spy writes, «They planned a secret rendezvous at midnight.» What does rendezvous mean here? | A hidden treasure | A long journey by boat | *A secret meeting at an agreed time and place | A code for a bank card
test: «The two spacecraft will rendezvous above the Earth tomorrow.» What does rendezvous mean here? | To be late for something | To dive under the water | *To meet and line up in space | To wrap a parcel
test: Which spelling is correct for the plural noun, meaning more than one meeting? | *rendezvous | rendezvouse | rendezvouses | rendezvouss
exam: «English uses whole French phrases: "rendezvous" (a meeting), "déjà vu" (already seen) and "bon appétit" (enjoy your meal).» Which French phrase means enjoy your meal? | a secret meeting | *bon appétit | rendezvous | déjà vu
